      <h2>About the film</h2>
      <p>
        It follows one hot season on the fringes of Mukundara: Indian grey wolves cooling off in a shrinking wetland,
        Bengal monitors wrestling, Sarus Cranes dancing, a chinkara looking for water, and the pitta pair raising chicks
        through the heatwave while waiting for the monsoon. The Arjuna and Indian ash sap come in around the
        eight-minute mark.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where it was filmed.</strong> The film was not shot inside the tiger reserve's forest. It was shot on
        the fringes, among the villages, farmland, waterbodies and grasslands that sit around Mukundara Hills Tiger
        Reserve in Kota district. These are working lands, shared by farmers, herders and wildlife. When I say
        "Mukundara" here I mean that wider landscape, not the reserve.
      </p>

